WEBVTT

NOTE
The Same Code, Charted Twice: the spoken lines. Dramatization with practice data; the voices are AI-generated.

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.010
Medic 1: V-fib! Charging to 200 joules.

2
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.180
Medic 1: Clear!

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.940
Medic 2: IV is in.

4
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.460
Medic 1: Still V-fib. Clear!

5
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.100
Medic 2: Epinephrine is in!

6
00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.470
Medic 1: Airway is in. Good waveform.

7
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.630
Medic 1: Clear!

8
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.450
Medic 2: Amiodarone, 300, is in.

9
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.050
Medic 1: When did we push the epi?

10
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.630
Medic 2: I can't read it. I think it was… 14:02?

11
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:23.810
Medic 1: Clear!

12
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:24.900
Medic 2: Epi is in!

13
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.070
Medic 1: Is that the fourth shock, or the fifth?

14
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.660
Medic 1: Everybody clear!

15
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.450
Medic 1: Did the amiodarone go in?

16
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.900
Medic 2: Three hundred, yes. This is the 150.

17
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.950
Medic 1: Organized rhythm. Pulse check.

18
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.970
Medic 2: Pulse! We've got ROSC.

19
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.360
Medic 1: BP 104 over 66. Inferior STEMI.

20
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.730
ED nurse: How many shocks? And when was the last epi?

21
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.240
Medic 2: Five, I think. I think the time was… 14:06?

22
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.030
Medic 2: ROSC… I think the time was… 14:10? 14:11?

23
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.480
Medic 2: Close enough.

24
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.030
Narrator: Now, the same code. With ARCTIN.

25
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.181
Every shock, every drug, every cycle: charted once, at the patient, with the time.

26
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.882
At each pulse check, the rhythm is one tap. The timers count from each dose.

27
00:01:10.932 --> 00:01:13.372
The decisions stay with your crew.

28
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.680
On the cabin display, the whole crew sees the shocks, the CPR clock, and every dose.

29
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.753
ARCTIN Hub builds the timeline, and the narrative, as you go.

30
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.133
With your agency's approval, the receiving hospital follows the code live.

31
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.757
The crew answers in ARCTIN Hub with a tap, and it lands in the chart.

32
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:56.391
ARCTIN records and reminds. Your crew makes every care decision.

33
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.151
So why would you chart any other way?

34
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.077
ARCTIN. Every second. Every life.
